    <h1>Arrows Puzzle Walkthrough: Escape Every Level with These Strategies</h1>

    <p>Arrows – Puzzle Escape looks simple: drag directional arrows around a grid until each one points at its target. Then level 40 hands you your first impossible-looking deadlock, and you realize this game is quietly one of the most elegant logic puzzles on mobile. This walkthrough covers everything you need — the general strategy that carries you through the early, middle, and late levels, the five most common stuck points and how to unstick them, and level progression tips that turn a casual player into someone who finishes packs without a single hint.</p>

    <p>Grab your board, and let's start pointing arrows.</p>

    <div class="disclaimer">Unofficial fan-made guide. Not affiliated with Ecffri.</div>

    <h2>What Is the Arrows Puzzle?</h2>
    <p>Arrows – Puzzle Escape is a logic-based sliding puzzle from developer Ecffri with more than 100 million installs. You are given an empty grid of cells, a set of arrow tiles, and a set of targets. Your job: place every arrow so that it points at exactly one target, using the empty cells as your canvas. The twist is that arrows move like sliding tiles — you can only slide them through empty space, you cannot lift them off the board, and locked tiles can never move. The result is a hybrid of a sliding puzzle and a logic deduction game, and it gets deeply strategic long before the mid-game.</p>

    <h2>General Arrows Puzzle Walkthrough Strategy</h2>
    <p>There is no single solution path through the game, but there is a reliable <em>method</em>. It changes shape as the levels get harder, so here is the strategy in three stages.</p>

    <h3>First 100 Levels: Learn the Placement Alphabet</h3>
    <div class="tip-card">
      <span class="pill pill-early">Early Game</span>
      <strong>Treat every level as a training exercise, not a race.</strong>
      <p>The first hundred levels introduce one concept at a time: straight shots, corner arrows, blocked cells, then colors. Play each one until you can explain <em>why</em> every arrow landed where it did. The discipline you build here is what makes later levels feel fair instead of random.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="tip-card">
      <span class="pill pill-early">Early Game</span>
      <strong>Find the forcing moves first.</strong>
      <p>In small grids, at least one arrow usually has exactly one possible landing spot — maybe because every other spot is blocked, or because another arrow already owns the space. Locate it, place it, and the board opens up. This is the single most important habit in the entire game.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="tip-card">
      <span class="pill pill-early">Early Game</span>
      <strong>Learn to read rows and columns like equations.</strong>
      <p>An arrow in the top row can only point right, left, or down. A corner arrow is even more constrained: it points in one of two directions. Every tile's position limits its legal directions, and counting those constraints is how you solve without guessing.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Middle Levels: Chain Reactions and Color Logic</h3>
    <div class="tip-card">
      <span class="pill pill-mid">Mid Game</span>
      <strong>Place colored locked arrows before anything else.</strong>
      <p>Once levels introduce colored arrows with locked tiles, color becomes a hard constraint: colored arrows must point at targets of their own color, and locked tiles never move. Solve the color structure first, then treat the rest of the board as a fresh (smaller) puzzle.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="tip-card">
      <span class="pill pill-mid">Mid Game</span>
      <strong>Plan chains backward — from target to arrow.</strong>
      <p>Many mid-game levels are chain triggers: moving one arrow unlocks a path for another, which unlocks a third. Instead of pushing tiles forward and hoping, pick the final target and ask: "What is the last arrow that can point at this target?" Work backward from that answer and the chain order becomes obvious.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="tip-card">
      <span class="pill pill-mid">Mid Game</span>
      <strong>Never move an arrow twice.</strong>
      <p>If you find yourself sliding the same tile back and forth, you are not solving — you are experimenting. Stop, undo to a clean state, and find the forced move you missed. A solved arrows level usually lets you place each arrow exactly once.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="tip-card">
      <span class="pill pill-mid">Mid Game</span>
      <strong>Use the edge of the board as a wall.</strong>
      <p>Grid borders are permanent walls, which makes edge-adjacent targets the easiest to satisfy — an arrow can only approach them from a small set of directions. Solving edges first narrows the interior dramatically.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Late Levels: Multi-Step Planning</h3>
    <div class="tip-card">
      <span class="pill pill-late">Late Game</span>
      <strong>Solve in layers: colors → edges → interior → leftovers.</strong>
      <p>Late boards can have 40+ arrows. Trying to solve them linearly is a recipe for deadlocks. Maintain a strict layer order — color constraints, then edge/corner tiles, then the interior, then any leftover special tiles — and you will rarely trap yourself.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="tip-card">
      <span class="pill pill-late">Late Game</span>
      <strong>Count occupancy like a Sudoku player.</strong>
      <p>Every empty cell must eventually hold an arrow (or be a permanent gap). If three arrows remain and only two legal cells remain, you have found a contradiction — one of your earlier placements is wrong. Backtrack with confidence instead of re-trying randomly.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="tip-card">
      <span class="pill pill-late">Late Game</span>
      <strong>Beware the self-blocking arrow.</strong>
      <p>The classic late-game trap: an arrow points at its target perfectly, but in doing so it blocks the sliding path of every arrow behind it. Before committing, trace the slide paths of the remaining arrows and make sure your "perfect" placement is not actually a wall.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="tip-card">
      <span class="pill pill-late">Late Game</span>
      <strong>Take breaks on big boards.</strong>
      <p>On 8x8 and larger grids, mental fatigue causes real mistakes. If you have stared at a board for more than five minutes, walk away for sixty seconds. A fresh look at the color layer usually reveals the forced move you kept skipping.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>Common Stuck Points in the Arrows Puzzle (and How to Unstick)</h2>
    <p>Getting stuck is not a sign of a bad player — it is a sign the level is doing its job. Here are the five situations that trap most players, and the exact trick that gets you out of each one.</p>

    <div class="stuck-grid">
      <div class="stuck-card">
        <span class="symptom">Scenario 1 — Arrow Deadlock</span>
        <h3>Two arrows, one legal cell, neither can move</h3>
        <p>You have two arrows left and only a single empty cell between them. This is a classic occupancy deadlock: you do not actually have enough space left for both arrows. Undo your most recent placement — one of your earlier arrows is blocking the path. Find the arrow that should have landed elsewhere and re-route it.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="stuck-card">
        <span class="symptom">Scenario 2 — Chain Trigger Order</span>
        <h3>Moving arrow A makes arrow B impossible</h3>
        <p>Chain levels are order-sensitive by design. If your chain collapses, the trigger order was wrong. Work backward: instead of asking which arrow to move first, ask which arrow <em>must</em> move last. That final arrow's landing spot dictates everything that came before it.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="stuck-card">
        <span class="symptom">Scenario 3 — Color Mismatch</span>
        <h3>Two same-colored targets, one matching arrow left</h3>
        <p>You counted wrong somewhere earlier — a colored arrow is sitting on the wrong target and "stealing" it. Retrace the color layer specifically. Every color on the board has a locked structure: arrows of a color must be countable against targets of that color. The mismatch reveals the bad placement.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="stuck-card">
        <span class="symptom">Scenario 4 — Path Blocking</span>
        <h3>Every arrow points correctly, but one cannot slide home</h3>
        <p>Your placements are logically correct but physically unreachable — the arrow's slide path is walled off. The fix is usually a reorder trick: slide a blocking arrow to a temporary spot, slide your target arrow home, then return the blocking arrow to its final position. Temporary park moves are a legitimate mid-game technique.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="stuck-card">
        <span class="symptom">Scenario 5 — The Last Arrow Paradox</span>
        <h3>One arrow left, one target left, but they cannot see each other</h3>
        <p>The final arrow is blocked by locked tiles or has no line of sight. This means your earlier placement of the <em>other</em> arrows is subtly wrong — probably a chain fired in the wrong order. Undo to the last color-placed arrow and replay the sequence with the final arrow's line of sight in mind.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2>Arrows Puzzle Escape Levels: Progression Tips</h2>
    <p>The game organizes levels into themed packs, and each pack has a personality. Here is how to move through them without burning hints.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Pack 1 (Tutorial):</strong> do not skip anything, even the levels you find trivial. They quietly install the habits (forcing moves, edge solving) you will need at level 200.</li>
      <li><strong>Color packs:</strong> before starting, count the arrows and targets per color. If the numbers do not balance, you know you misread the board.</li>
      <li><strong>Big-grid packs:</strong> use the layer method religiously. Edges first, then corners, then the interior. The board may look like a wall of arrows, but it still falls apart one forced move at a time.</li>
      <li><strong>Special-tile packs (teleporters, reflectors):</strong> treat the special tile as a new wall type, and solve around it exactly like a locked tile. The special tile rarely changes the logic — it only changes the geometry.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>If a pack feels like a brick wall, that is the game telling you a mechanic you skimmed has become load-bearing. Go back to the first level that used that mechanic and replay it until it is effortless.</p>

